pop onto the website for feel good  subscription box firm all true and you  might think the company is still  operating as normal but there's one very  important detail the company hasn't  communicated to potential customers all  true is going out of business  build as a way to support small mostly  women-run businesses all true never  announced it was closing its virtual  doors  frustrated subscribers are still waiting  on orders they've already paid for while  vendors say they're sitting on unpaid  invoices totaling at least 1.9 million  dollars the los angeles-based company  laid off its entire staff to about 50  earlier this month according to a  linkedin post from a former employee  around the same time all true filed  paperwork to start an assignment for the  benefit of creditors a voluntary  alternative to formal bankruptcy  since these cases don't require a public  court filing in california this step can  be taken by distressed businesses  looking to minimize publicity  ceo matt richardson did not make a  public statement when the company  decided to shudder but confirmed to  forbes last week that all true is in the  process of an assignment for the benefit  of creditors  richardson said he can't comment further  but said quote the assignee hopes to  have an update soon at which point there  will be more information to share  richardson co-founded what is now all  true with his childhood friend brett  mccullum in 2014 it developed a legion  of loyal fans who joined facebook groups  dedicated to discussing selling and  trading their all true items with other  subscribers touting how much they were  helping local artisans both founders  landed on the 2019 forbes 30 under 30  social impact list they changed their  name to altru in 2021  last year the company claimed to have  more than 300 thousand subscribers each  paying anywhere between 50 to 55 dollars  per box  despite their image as promoting small  businesses according to vendors on  reddit the company hasn't been very  helpful companies expecting to be  featured in all true this year never got  paid for the products they sent leaving  at least one small business out of four  hundred thousand dollars and on the  verge of insolvency  seven brands say they are owed one  hundred thousand dollars or more  five vendors told forbes their attempts  to contact all true have been met with  silence  though these businesses can file a claim  as one of all true's creditors it  remains unclear if they will ever get a  payout or recover their products some of  which are handmade and one of a kind  forbes found 18 brands claiming they  haven't been paid by all true altogether  the vendors say they lost 170 000 units  of unpaid product from their businesses  customers too are furious  altru gave shoppers the option of paying  for a single box or they could purchase  an annual subscription of 4 boxes for  roughly 200  altru also offered additional products  as voluntary add-ons each quarter  while all true's demise seems sudden  this isn't the first time it has faced  scrutiny when it was still named  causebox the company voluntarily  recalled stainless steel kettles sent to  customers in early 2021 because they  posed a burn hazard  according to the consumer product safety  commission the company received 122  reports of the kettles releasing hot  water or excessive steam resulting in 18  minor burn injuries  at the time the company admitted to  sourcing the kettle's in-house while  sticking the name of brooklyn-based  artisan brand rose and fitzgerald on the  bottom of the kettle as part of a  licensing agreement  [Music]
 

